<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=vulcanization">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Chemistry</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vulcanization</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Vulcanisation, Curing</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vulcanization is the hardening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>, originally of natural rubber treated with sulfur and heat, which forms cross-links between its polymer chains and makes it firmer and more durable. Ancient Mesoamerican cultures already cured rubber with sulfur-rich plant juices, an early form of the process. Charles Goodyear, who worked in the 1830s to strengthen rubber, found in 1839 that a mixture of rubber and sulfur dropped on a hot pan hardened instead of melting, and by 1844 he had patented the process and produced the rubber on an industrial scale. Thomas Hancock had taken out a British patent eight weeks earlier, in November 1843.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vulcanization.jpg" alt="Vulcanization" width="330" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred T. Palmer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:VulcanizationMold1941.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1839</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>United States</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Charles Goodyear · Thomas Hancock</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Thomas Hancock took out a British patent for vulcanization with sulfur on 21 November 1843, eight weeks before Goodyear’s US patent of 30 January 1844; accounts differ on whether Hancock had studied samples of Goodyear’s rubber.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">Rubber<span>c. 1600 BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=vulcanization">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
